    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. can u go into a bit more detail on the signaling mechanism? like how exactly does leucine act as a signal to the cell to initiate that mTORC1 response? i want to be able to explain the cellular mechanics clearly to my client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Leucine activates the mTORC1 pathway primarily through its interaction with a family of proteins known as the leucine-rich signaling motifs (LRR), found in proteins such as RHEB and RPTOR. When leucine levels are sufficient, leucine is transported into the cell and modulates the activity of the 4E-binding proteins (4E-BPs), which then inhibits eIF3F complex formation necessary for initiating protein synthesis by ribosomes. Leucine removes this inhibition, therefore, promoting the initiation phase of protein translation.\n\n\nSimultaneously, leucine-mediated activation strengthens Rheb-GTPase and inhibits Rheb-GDPase, further promoting the GTP-bound active state of mTORC1, which is crucial for its activation. Upon activation, mTORC1 stimulates downstream targets that drive protein synthesis, ribosome biogenesis, ribosome maturation, and the lipid biosynthesis necessary for muscle cell growth and recovery, contributing to muscle hypertrophy.",
      "source": "target",
